WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.339 --> 00:00:06.430
In February 2020, Elon Musk reportedly rallied
his workers at the south Texas factory where

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:10.370
SpaceX is building Starship at one o’clock
in the morning

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:15.170
The SpaceX CEO wanted to know why the factory
wasn’t running at all hours of the night.

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:17.140
Musk has always wanted to move fast.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:21.960
It is evident in how quickly he wants to build
Starships - designed to be a fully reusable

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:25.450
launch vehicle that will be the largest object
to ever fly.

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:29.160
This is, I think, the most inspiring thing that I’ve ever seen.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.910
When the Starship spacecraft is stacked on
top of the Super Heavy booster, it will tower

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:38.100
over NASA’s monster Saturn V that sent astronauts
to the moon - with roughly twice its thrust

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.340
and weight.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:42.700
Anyone standing beside Starship would look
pretty small as I found out.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:46.250
You can hardly see me right now unless we
zoom in really close.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:49.440
Musk has said SpaceX intends to build one
Starship a week.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.110
And eventually, one every three days.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:55.924
To put that into perspective, it can take
years to build a single rocket.

00:00:55.924 --> 00:00:59.441
Boeing spent about 5 years constructing just one core stage 

00:00:59.441 --> 00:01:02.898
of NASA's latest launch vehicle in development, the SLS.

00:01:02.898 --> 00:01:06.990
So, why does SpaceX need so many Starships?

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:11.579
Musk believes his vision of colonizing Mars
requires a fleet of a thousand ships.

00:01:11.579 --> 00:01:16.179
Building one every three days would produce about
100 a year, allowing SpaceX to reach its goal

00:01:16.179 --> 00:01:17.310
in a decade.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:22.479
The 1,000-strong fleet, each capable
of carrying 100 people, could transport 100,000

00:01:22.479 --> 00:01:26.840
settlers or 100 megatons of cargo to Mars
in the first year.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.329
SpaceX wouldn’t make trips to Mars every
year, however.

00:01:29.329 --> 00:01:33.669
It would only launch the fleet when the orbits
of Earth and Mars are closest to each other

00:01:33.669 --> 00:01:37.240
in order to shorten the trip which happens
roughly every two years.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:43.880
Still, it’s a 34 million mile (55 million
km) trek that takes about six months one way.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:48.899
The goal of SpaceX is to send a million people
to Mars by 2050.

00:01:48.899 --> 00:01:52.356
Musk is confident that moving there will be
similar to the cost of selling a home in the

00:01:52.356 --> 00:01:53.932
United States.

00:01:53.932 --> 00:01:58.789
He pegs a round trip ticket at half a million
dollars one day and even less in the future.

00:01:58.789 --> 00:02:03.109
Relying on ticket sales alone won’t pay
for that many Starships which is why SpaceX

00:02:03.109 --> 00:02:07.219
is creating a satellite internet service for
Earth called Starlink.

00:02:07.219 --> 00:02:11.680
Musk has predicted the broadband internet
system could bring in as much as $50 billion

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.290
dollars a year.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:16.439
The Falcon 9 has been responsible for launching
the satellites.

00:02:16.439 --> 00:02:20.750
It’s no Starship because it’s not fully
reusable but it is a step in that direction

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:22.620
since it can be partially reused.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:27.280
The booster lands, gets turned around in a
few months, and takes off again, though that’s

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.030
still not good enough for Musk.

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:31.659
I wouldn’t call it rapid by aircraft
standards.

00:02:31.659 --> 00:02:35.879
On the other hand, Starship is intended to
be turned around as fast as today’s passenger

00:02:35.879 --> 00:02:40.239
planes with minimal maintenance and refueling
before it takes off again.

00:02:40.239 --> 00:02:45.299
This is the fundamental holy grail breakthrough
needed for access to space, to make humanity

00:02:45.299 --> 00:02:46.629
a true space-faring civilization.

00:02:46.629 --> 00:02:50.190
We must have a fully and rapidly reusable
rocket.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:55.260
That includes a fully and rapidly reusable
booster that kicks Starship into orbit, separates,

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.420
and returns to Earth within minutes.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:02.749
The booster is only required to escape Earth’s
deep gravity well so it’s not needed on Mars.

00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:04.381
One idea is to catch the Super Heavy with

00:03:04.381 --> 00:03:07.249
the launch tower arm - a bold and still untested

00:03:07.249 --> 00:03:08.820
strategy before putting it back into 

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.110
service as much as a dozen times a day.

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:13.750
We have yet to see the booster in action.

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:16.920
Only prototypes of the spacecraft have flown
on their own.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.499
But that could soon change.

00:03:18.499 --> 00:03:22.700
SpaceX has asked regulators to approve its
first orbital flight test of the Starship

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:24.270
and Super Heavy together.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:29.200
And the combination could also take off from
an ocean spaceport in 2022.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.930
SpaceX purchased a couple of oil rigs, intending
to transform them into floating Starship platforms.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:38.650
They’re named Deimos and Phobos after the
two Martian moons, given the ambitions of

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:39.670
the company.

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:44.280
The aim is to send an uncrewed flight to the
red planet in 2024 with humans potentially

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.677
traveling there for the first time in 2026.

00:03:47.677 --> 00:03:50.889
SpaceX is also working to turn Starship into a lunar lander

00:03:50.889 --> 00:03:52.859
to send astronauts back to the moon.

00:03:52.859 --> 00:03:57.719
Before any of these achievements, Musk is
bracing for production hell.

00:03:57.719 --> 00:04:03.700
He survived it with Tesla in 2017 and 2018
- toiling over how to build cars faster, spending

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:08.590
nights sleeping at his factory, before ultimately
overcoming the manufacturing nightmare to

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:11.760
deliver nearly half a million vehicles in
2020.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:16.639
What the entrepreneur is doing with cars he
wants to do with rockets.

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:21.215
Musk claims an assembly line of reusable Starships
would drive down the cost of a launch to 

00:04:21.215 --> 00:04:23.550
$2 million dollars, aspirationally.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:29.080
Compared to the estimated $2 billion per launch
of the non-reusable SLS.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:34.199
Reducing the cost of space flight is necessary
if life were to ever be multi-planetary.

00:04:34.199 --> 00:04:39.737
For Musk, a city on Mars is not only an insurance
policy against a potential catastrophe on Earth.

00:04:39.737 --> 00:04:41.819
For him, it’s much more than that.

00:04:41.819 --> 00:04:45.810
Do we want to have a future that is inspiring
and exciting?

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:51.500
A future where we are a space-faring civilization,
and out there among the stars, I think that's,

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:54.150
every kid gets excited about that, you don’t
even need to teach them.

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:58.509
Shooting for the stars begins with a plan
to rapidly churn out Starships here in south

00:04:58.509 --> 00:05:02.550
Texas which has been aptly named: Starbase.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:06.669
Starship is designed to fly two or three times
a day and could also transport people from point

00:05:06.669 --> 00:05:08.440
to point on Earth.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:13.110
SpaceX says a trip from Sydney to London would
be just 51 minutes.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:17.960
If that becomes a reality, it would drastically
reshape our concept of saving time.

